            <!-- 4. Reconciliation & Results Layer -->
            <section class="content-section" id="reconciliation-layer">
                <h2>3. Reconciliation & Compliance Results Layer</h2>
                <p>
                    Reconciliation runs produce definitive calculation summaries that populate audit dashboards and executive reports.
                </p>

                <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                    <table class="data-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Table Name</th>
                                <th>DB Table</th>
                                <th>Primary Role & Calculation Function</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Reconciliation Result</strong></td>
                                <td><code>samp_reconciliation_result</code></td>
                                <td>Header record for a complete reconciliation run. Stores run timestamp, scope (All Publishers vs Single Vendor), total enterprise true-up cost, and overall compliance percentage.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>License Metric Result</strong></td>
                                <td><code>samp_license_metric_result</code></td>
                                <td>Stores the granular compliance output for a specific software model and metric. Contains: <code>rights_owned</code>, <code>rights_used</code>, <code>over_under</code> (delta), and <code>true_up_cost</code>.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>License Position</strong></td>
                                <td><code>samp_sw_license_position</code></td>
                                <td>Aggregates compliance at the publisher and product family level for reporting to CFO and CIO leadership.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Remediation Option</strong></td>
                                <td><code>samp_remediation_option</code></td>
                                <td>Generated automatically when <code>over_under < 0</code>. Formulates actionable choices: Purchase Rights, Remove Unlicensed, or Create Allocations.</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 5. Reclamation & SaaS Layer -->
            <section class="content-section" id="reclamation-layer">
                <h2>4. Reclamation, SaaS & Spend Optimization Layer</h2>
                <p>
                    Tables governing automated license de-provisioning, usage thresholds, and SaaS subscriber integrations.
                </p>

                <div class="grid-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Reclamation Rules (samp_sw_reclamation_rule)</div>
                        <p>
                            Defines corporate policy thresholds for software harvesting:
                        </p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>days_unused</code>: Number of days without an application launch (e.g. 60 or 90 days).</li>
                            <li><code>total_usage_threshold_hours</code>: Minimum cumulative runtime required.</li>
                            <li><code>notify_user</code>: Boolean flag triggering employee email or Microsoft Teams prompt.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Reclamation Candidates (samp_sw_reclamation_candidate)</div>
                        <p>
                            Represents an individual target for license harvesting:
                        </p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>user</code> & <code>device</code>: The specific employee and machine targeted.</li>
                            <li><code>state</code>: Attention Required → User Notified → Awaiting Approval → Removal in Progress → Closed Complete.</li>
                            <li><code>potential_savings</code>: Projected dollar savings reclaimed back to the budget.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-card info-card--tip" style="margin-top: 16px;">
                    <div class="info-card-title">SaaS Subscriptions (samp_sw_subscription)</div>
                    <p>
                        Tracks direct API integrations for cloud services (Microsoft 365, Salesforce, Adobe Creative Cloud, Zoom). Stores <code>user_email</code>, <code>assigned_plan</code>, <code>last_login_date</code>, and <code>activity_status</code> to identify candidates for license tier downgrades (e.g., M365 E5 to E3).
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- 6. Hardware Topology -->
            <section class="content-section" id="hardware-topology">
                <h2>Hardware & Virtualization Topology in Server Licensing</h2>
                <p>
                    One of the most complex aspects of SAM Pro is calculating compliance for server software like <strong>Microsoft SQL Server</strong> or <strong>Oracle Database</strong>, which are licensed based on underlying hardware compute capacity rather than install counts.
                </p>

                <div class="diagram-block">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Hardware Topology Hierarchy & Core Multipliers</div>
                    <div class="mermaid">
                    graph TD
                        VC["vCenter / Cloud Datacenter<br/>cmdb_ci_vcenter_datacenter"] --> CL["Cluster Entity<br/>cmdb_ci_cluster"]
                        CL --> ESX1["ESXi Host 1<br/>cmdb_ci_esx_server<br/>2 Sockets x 16 Cores = 32 Cores"]
                        CL --> ESX2["ESXi Host 2<br/>cmdb_ci_esx_server<br/>2 Sockets x 16 Cores = 32 Cores"]
                        
                        ESX1 --> VM1["Virtual Machine 1 (8 vCPUs)<br/>Hosts MS SQL 2019"]
                        ESX1 --> VM2["Virtual Machine 2 (4 vCPUs)<br/>Hosts App Server"]
                        ESX2 --> VM3["Virtual Machine 3 (16 vCPUs)<br/>Hosts Oracle 19c"]

                        classDef cluster fill:#1e1e2f,stroke:#8b5cf6,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff
                        classDef host fill:#1e1e2f,stroke:#3b82f6,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff
                        classDef vm fill:#1e1e2f,stroke:#10b981,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff

                        class VC,CL cluster
                        class ESX1,ESX2 host
                        class VM1,VM2,VM3 vm
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                    <div class="info-card-title">Why Hardware CMDB Integrity is Critical for SAM</div>
                    <p>
                        If Discovery fails to populate the relationship between a Virtual Machine (<code>cmdb_ci_vm_instance</code>) and its parent ESXi Host (<code>cmdb_ci_esx_server</code>), the SAM Reconciliation Engine cannot calculate Per Core or Per Processor metrics accurately. 
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        For instance, Microsoft SQL Server requires a minimum of 4 core licenses per virtual machine. If the cluster topology is unknown, the engine cannot determine whether it is cheaper to license the virtual machines individually or license all physical cores in the cluster with Software Assurance (SA) for unlimited virtualization rights.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>
    
        </div>
    </div>
</main>

